We have analyzed the reconstruction of $B$ decays into a $ho$ meson and a lepton-neutrino pair, utilizing the hadronic products of the opposing $B$ meson decay to enhance signal detection. The analysis is based on 2019-2021 Belle II data, yielding 1.34 fb$^{-1}$ of integrated luminosity. The $ho$ meson was identified through the decay into a proton and a negatively charged pion, or a kaon, with the recoil $B$ meson decay products used to isolate the signal. The results show that 10.3, 10.2, and 1.0 B decays were reconstructed for $B^0$ decays into $ho^+ \ell^-  ar{
u}_\ell$ with protons and pions, and $B^0$ decays into $ho^0 \ell^-  ar{
u}_\ell$ with pions, respectively. The 95% confidence interval for the branching fraction of $B^0$ decays into $ho^0 \ell^-  ar{
u}_\ell$ is 1.15e-5 to 1.25e-5, with the latter result being consistent with the world average. The reconstruction of the recoil $B$ meson also enabled the measurement of the differential decay rate d$\Gamma$/dq$^
